Why a released APK is GPL-3.0
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The app's native library (libdigitalpet-native.so) links espeak-ng, which is
licensed GPL-3.0-or-later, and the app bundles espeak-ng's phoneme data under
android/app/src/main/assets/espeak-ng-data/. espeak-ng is used by Piper for
text-to-phoneme conversion; the pet cannot speak without it.

Apache-2.0 is one-way compatible with the GPL: Apache-2.0 code may be combined
into a GPL-3.0 work, and the result is GPL-3.0. Consequently ANY BINARY THAT
LINKS espeak-ng -- including a released APK -- must be distributed under
GPL-3.0, with the corresponding source and licence text made available to
recipients.

The obligation attaches to linking, not to calling. Piper does provide a
non-espeak phonemizer (PhonemeType::TextPhonemes), but choosing it changes
nothing while espeak-ng remains in the link, and nearly every published Piper
voice is an espeak-phoneme model.

The ESP32-S3 firmware is unaffected. It never links espeak-ng; the audio it
plays arrives over BLE already synthesised.

PolyMO's own source files remain Apache-2.0 and may be reused under those
terms independently of the combined binary.

