The three marked NOT RECORDED were obtained without leaving a version behind --
no tag, no commit, no manifest. They are all permissively licensed, so nothing
turns on it legally, but a build cannot be reproduced exactly until they are
pinned, and that is a known gap rather than an oversight nobody noticed.

The espeak-ng commit is the one that carries an obligation: GPL-3.0 requires
the corresponding source of the linked library be available to anyone who
receives the binary, and the commit above is what makes that identifiable.

